# Target C-II — the high-`t` complete rectangle has common order and inverse-`D` phase width

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** high-`t` complete rectangle; canonical global strict-rhombus angular-defect budget; canonical train displacement cones and signed monotone-grid order lemma  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_complete_rectangle_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The high-`t` complete-rectangle theorem gives internally noncrossing train families

\[
A_*\times B_*,
\]

with

\[
|A_*|>0.0000226D-o(D),
\qquad
|B_*|>0.3134D-o(D),
\]

such that every cross-pair is one strict rhombus. The rows `A_*` lie in one shrinking dominant projective phase window.

This note upgrades that combinatorial rectangle to an ordered, near-triangular grid. After deleting only `O(1)` orientation-ambiguous columns, choosing one of the two oriented crossing sides, and then trimming at most half of each shore, there are subfamilies

\[
A_0\subset A_* ,
\qquad
B_0\subset B_*
\]

with

\[
\boxed{|A_0|>0.0000112D-o(D),}
\tag{HP.1}
\]

\[
\boxed{|B_0|>0.0783D-o(D),}
\tag{HP.2}
\]

for which

\[
\boxed{
{\pi\over3}<\theta_{ab}
<{\pi\over3}+{7.7\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1})
\qquad(a\in A_0,b\in B_0).
}
\tag{HP.3}
\]

Both families have internal projective phase spread `O(1/D)`, and every row sees the columns in one common order while every column sees the rows in one common order.

Thus the current `t<=1.8687` survivor contains an actual common-order, inverse-diameter-width oblique train grid of size `Theta(D) x Theta(D)`.

---

## 1. Remove the orientation-ambiguous columns

Let the row phases in `A_*` lie in a shrinking projective window of width

\[
\omega_D=o(1)
\]

about the dominant atom `phi`.

For `b in B_*`, every crossing angle with a row belongs to

\[
\left({\pi\over3},{\pi\over2}\right].
\]

Relative to the row window, there are two possible oriented branches, clockwise and counterclockwise from `phi`.

Call a column **ambiguous** if its acute representative uses different oriented branches for two rows of `A_*`. Since all row phases differ by at most `omega_D`, an ambiguous column must lie within `O(omega_D)` of the projective direction orthogonal to `phi`. Consequently every one of its crossings with `A_*` has angle

\[
\theta_{ab}\ge {\pi\over2}-O(\omega_D),
\]

and therefore angular defect

\[
\theta_{ab}-{\pi\over3}
\ge {\pi\over6}-o(1).
\tag{HP.4}
\]

The canonical global defect budget is

\[
\sum_R\left(\theta_R-{\pi\over3}\right)
<C_{phase}D,
\qquad
C_{phase}={\pi^2\sqrt3(2+\sqrt3)\over12}.
\tag{HP.5}
\]

One ambiguous column has more than `0.0000226D-o(D)` crossings with `A_*`, so by (HP.4) it spends `Omega(D)` defect. Hence (HP.5) permits only `O(1)` ambiguous columns.

Delete them. The remaining `>0.3134D-o(D)` columns split into two classes according to their consistent oriented crossing side. One class, call it `B_+`, satisfies

\[
\boxed{|B_+|>0.1567D-o(D).}
\tag{HP.6}
\]

Reverse all orientations if necessary. We may choose real phase representatives

\[
\alpha_a\quad(a\in A_*),
\qquad
\beta_b\quad(b\in B_+)
\]

so that every cross-pair obeys

\[
{\pi\over3}<\beta_b-\alpha_a<{2\pi\over3}.
\tag{HP.7}
\]

The actual acute rhombus angle is represented on this common branch; after the `O(1)` ambiguous deletion no sign change occurs across the shrinking row window.

---

## 2. The complete product spends only `O(D)` angular defect

For `a in A_*`, `b in B_+`, put

\[
\delta_{ab}:=\beta_b-\alpha_a-{\pi\over3}>0.
\tag{HP.8}
\]

Every `A_* x B_+` pair is a distinct strict rhombus, so by (HP.5)

\[
S:=\sum_{a\in A_*}\sum_{b\in B_+}\delta_{ab}
<C_{phase}D.
\tag{HP.9}
\]

The safe shore sizes give

\[
|A_*||B_+|
>(0.0000226)(0.1567)D^2-o(D^2).
\]

Define the average defect

\[
\lambda_D={S\over |A_*||B_+|}.
\]

The exact certificate proves

\[
\boxed{
\lambda_D
<{1.502\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{HP.10}
\]

---

## 3. Product structure collapses both phase spreads

Set

\[
\alpha^+=\max_{a\in A_*}\alpha_a,
\qquad
\beta^-=\min_{b\in B_+}\beta_b,
\]

\[
g=\beta^- -\alpha^+-{\pi\over3}>0,
\]

\[
r_a=\alpha^+-\alpha_a\ge0,
\qquad
q_b=\beta_b-\beta^-\ge0.
\]

Then exactly

\[
\boxed{
\delta_{ab}=g+r_a+q_b.
}
\tag{HP.11}
\]

Averaging over the complete product gives

\[
\lambda_D
=g+{1\over|A_*|}\sum_ar_a
+{1\over|B_+|}\sum_bq_b.
\tag{HP.12}
\]

All three terms are nonnegative. Hence

\[
g\le\lambda_D,
\qquad
{1\over|A_*|}\sum_ar_a\le\lambda_D,
\qquad
{1\over|B_+|}\sum_bq_b\le\lambda_D.
\tag{HP.13}

By Markov retain at least half of each shore:

\[
A_0=\{a:r_a\le2\lambda_D\},
\qquad
B_0=\{b:q_b\le2\lambda_D\}.
\tag{HP.14}
\]

Then

\[
|A_0|\ge|A_*|/2>0.0000112D-o(D),
\]

\[
|B_0|\ge|B_+|/2>0.0783D-o(D),
\]

and, using (HP.11),

\[
0<\delta_{ab}
\le g+2\lambda_D+2\lambda_D
\le5\lambda_D.
\]

The exact certificate verifies

\[
5{C_{phase}\over(0.0000226)(0.1567)}
<7.7\times10^6.
\]

This proves (HP.3).

Equations (HP.14) also show directly that the row and column phase spreads are each at most

\[
2\lambda_D=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{HP.15}
\]

---

## 4. Common crossing order

The signed monotone-grid order argument used by the lower-`t` rectangle theorem depends only on:

1. two internally noncrossing train families;
2. complete mutual crossing;
3. one common real orientation satisfying (HP.7).

All three hold for `A_0,B_0`.

Canonical train geometry gives a common projection direction along which every selected train centerline is strictly monotone. Every `A_0 x B_0` crossing has the same determinant sign. Therefore the signed monotone-grid lemma yields

\[
\boxed{
\text{all rows meet the columns in one common order,}
}
\tag{HP.16}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\text{all columns meet the rows in one common order.}
}
\tag{HP.17}
\]

Thus `A_0 x B_0` is a genuine ordered pseudoline rectangle, not only a complete bipartite crossing graph.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The high-`t` branch through `1.8687` now contains

```text
>0.0000112 D row trains
x
>0.0783 D column trains
complete mutual strict-rhombus crossing
+ both shores internally noncrossing
+ one common crossing order on both shores
+ each shore has projective phase spread O(1/D)
+ every grid angle = pi/3 + O(1/D)
  (explicitly < pi/3 + 7.7e6/D + o(1/D)).
```

The large numerical constant reflects the very thin guaranteed row shore at the current endpoint, but the scale is the important point: across a linear-by-linear rectangle, the total phase drift is bounded in absolute coordinates.

The next closing target is no longer to find a grid. It is to combine this ordered near-triangular rectangle with the finite-defect/KKT strip and the common diameter hub, so that one adjacent row pair has both a bounded active-diameter interface and a macroscopic ordered transverse scaffold. That is the natural setting for a source-preserving local multishear or a forbidden diameter-chord pattern.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed here.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only high-`t` common-order/inverse-`D` phase-collapse rectangle through `t<=1.8687`. C-II and Level I remain open.
